**user**:
> In dplyr, I want to do something like:
> df4 <- df3 %>% mutate(mu=mu * 1e6 / gdp.2015.gbp)
> But I want to do that for an arbitrary column, rather than `mu`, specified by a string. How do I do that?
**assistant**:
> To modify a column specified by a string within a `dplyr::mutate` operation, you can use the `mutate()` function along with `sym()` and the `:=` operator. Here's how you can achieve that:
> 
> ```r
> library(dplyr)
> library(rlang)
> 
> # Assume `column_name` is the name of the column you want to modify
> column_name <- "mu"  # This can be dynamically set to any column name
> 
> df4 <- df3 %>%
>   mutate(!!sym(column_name) := !!sym(column_name) * 1e6 / gdp.2015.gbp)
> ```
> 
> In this code:
> 
> - `!!` (bang-bang operator) is used with `sym(column_name)` to unquote and evaluate the symbol corresponding to the string.
> - The `:=` operator allows you to refer to a column name programmatically within `mutate()`.
> 
> By using this approach, you can easily specify any column by changing the `column_name` variable.
